.TH TUI_TEXTEDIT_INIT 3 2026-10-05 "libtuikit 0.1" "libtuikit Manual"
.SH NAME
tui_textedit_init, tui_textedit_set_text, tui_textedit_insert,
tui_textedit_handle, tui_textedit_lines, tui_textedit_cursor,
tui_textedit_render, tui_textedit_summary \- multi-line text editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <tuikit/textedit.h>
.PP
.BI "void   tui_textedit_init(tui_textedit *" te ", char *" buf ", size_t " size ", const char *" initial );
.BI "void   tui_textedit_set_text(tui_textedit *" te ", const char *" text );
.BI "size_t tui_textedit_insert(tui_textedit *" te ", const char *" s ", size_t " n );
.BI "int    tui_textedit_handle(tui_textedit *" te ", const tui_event *" ev );
.BI "int    tui_textedit_lines(const tui_textedit *" te );
.BI "void   tui_textedit_cursor(const tui_textedit *" te ", int *" row ", int *" col );
.BI "void   tui_textedit_render(tui_textedit *" te ", tui_canvas " c ", tui_style " st );
.BI "int    tui_textedit_summary(const char *" text ", int " cols ", char *" out ", size_t " outsz );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A
.B tui_textedit
edits several lines of UTF-8 text in a buffer the caller owns \(em an issue
description, a message body.
Unlike
.BR tui_lineedit_init (3),
it keeps every newline and tab, typed or pasted.
It is a pure model: an event in, the text changed; drawing is separate.
.PP
Positions are byte offsets on character boundaries; a combining mark moves
and is deleted with its character.
Columns are display columns: a wide character takes two, a combining mark
none, a tab reaches the next multiple of eight.
Lines are not wrapped: the view scrolls horizontally as well as vertically,
so a column on the screen is a column in the text.
.PP
.BR tui_textedit_init ()
starts editing
.I buf
(capacity
.IR size ,
NUL included) holding
.I initial
(may be NULL), with the cursor at the start.
.BR tui_textedit_set_text ()
replaces the text the same way and does not count as an edit:
.I te\->dirty
is set by edits only, so a program can tell whether the user changed
anything.
.PP
.BR tui_textedit_insert ()
inserts
.I n
bytes at the cursor: CR LF and a lone CR become a newline, tab and newline
are kept, other control characters are dropped, malformed UTF-8 becomes
U+FFFD, and what does not fit is left out at a character boundary with
.I te\->truncated
set.
.SS Keys
.BR tui_textedit_handle ()
returns 1 for an event it used and 0 for one it leaves to the program (Esc,
Ctrl-S, Shift-Tab, other Ctrl and Alt chords).
.TS
l l.
characters, paste	insert
Enter, Tab	newline, tab
Left, Right	a character
Up, Down	a line *
PgUp, PgDn	a page *
Home, End	line start, end
Ctrl-A, Ctrl-E	line start, end
Ctrl+Home, Ctrl+End	text start, end
Backspace, Delete	a character **
Ctrl-K	rest of the line **
.TE
.PP
* keeping the column aimed for; a page is the height last drawn.
** a newline counts as a character: deleting it joins two lines, and
Ctrl-K at the end of a line removes the newline.
.SS Drawing
.BR tui_textedit_render ()
scrolls so the cursor is in view, draws the visible lines into the canvas
with the tabs expanded and control characters as U+00B7, clears the rest
with
.IR st ,
and puts the terminal cursor at the cursor.
The height it drew at is the step of PgUp and PgDn.
.BR tui_textedit_lines ()
and
.BR tui_textedit_cursor ()
report the number of lines and the cursor's line and display column, for a
status line.
.PP
.BR tui_textedit_summary ()
writes one line summarising a text for a form row: its first line, cut to
.I cols
columns with "\[u2026]", and " (N lines)" when there is more than one.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR tui_textedit_insert ()
returns the bytes inserted.
.BR tui_textedit_summary ()
returns the columns the summary takes, or \-1 when
.I outsz
is too small.
.SH NOTES
issue-manager's own text-area widget is where this one comes from; its
names (and its summary function's) are left to issue-manager, so a program
can link both.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tui_lineedit_init (3),
.BR tui_fieldset_init (3),
.BR libtuikit (7)
